---
import AppShell from "../layouts/AppShell.astro";
import Callout from "../components/ui/Callout.astro";
import { allChapters, chaptersByModule } from "../lib/chapters";

const chapters = await allChapters();
const grouped = chaptersByModule(chapters);
---

<AppShell title="How to study this · Inside Book a Slot" description="A practical study plan for Inside Book a Slot — a code-grounded study guide to the FastAPI assessment.">
  <article class="chapter-content">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="page-eyebrow">Before you begin</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">How to study this site</h1>
      <p class="page-lead">
        A code-grounded study guide to the FastAPI assessment. It pays off in the order it's designed.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>Who this is for</h2>
    <p>
      You are comfortable with JavaScript, TypeScript, React, Node.js and REST APIs, but you have
      limited recent hands-on experience with Python backends. This site assumes exactly that:
      it explains Python and FastAPI concepts as they appear, always anchored to real files in the
      repository.
    </p>

    <h2>The suggested rhythm</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One chapter per sitting</strong> — each is 7–14 minutes plus a quiz. Stop at the quiz.</li>
      <li><strong>Read, then play.</strong> Every chapter ends in a self-check. Replay the ones you miss tomorrow.</li>
      <li><strong>Follow the module order.</strong> It is sequenced: orientation → language → data → identity → domain → running → confidence → reference → beyond.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you have very little time</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      To survive a technical conversation about this project, read chapters 8, 9 and 10 —
      they contain the decisions that actually matter: the slot lifecycle, the concurrency-safe
      claim, and the review/Redis queue.
    </p>

    <h2>What is real here (the ground rules)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every code block is a <strong>frozen copy</strong> from <code>src/code-snapshots</code>, taken at the verified commits.</li>
      <li>Every claim is checked against the repo. Where the site says "this isn't implemented", it means the repository does not do it — e.g. no Redis worker, no concurrent stress test.</li>
      <li>Where a design is a deliberate assessment choice (a pending slot <em>is</em> a bookings row; tokens are stored in the DB), the <em>why</em> is explained from the repo's own decision log.</li>
    </ul>

    <Callout kind="note" title="This site runs independently">
      Source lives entirely under <code>study-site/</code> in the public <code>book-a-slot</code> repository,
      never touches the assessment API, and is not part of the submission. It is a learning aid.
    </Callout>

    <h2>The 20 chapters, by module</h2>
    {grouped.map((g) => (
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-400">{g.module.number}</p>
        <h3 class="mt-1 text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">{g.module.label}</h3>
        <ol class="mt-2 space-y-1">
          {g.items.map((c) => (
            <li class="text-sm">
              <a href={`/chapters/${c.id}`} class="font-medium text-indigo-700 underline decoration-indigo-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-indigo-900">
                {String(c.chapter).padStart(2, "0")} · {c.title}
              </a>
              <span class="text-slate-500"> — {c.summary}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    ))}

    <h2>When should I stop reading and start doing?</h2>
    <p>
      After chapter 11. Fire up the real stack and run the labs at the end of chapter 20.
      The fast way to confuse yourself: try to build a small change (e.g. add a
      <code>cancelled</code> endpoint) and see how each chapter's reasoning blocks or
      enables it. Making — then debugging — that attempt beats re-reading repeatedly.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href="/chapters/01-overview" class="btn btn-primary">
        Begin at chapter 01
      </a>
      <a href="/playground" class="btn btn-secondary">
        Skip to the playground
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</AppShell>